<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anne of Cleves</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1515">1515</a>–<a href="/years/1557">1557</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-la-marck">House of La Marck</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a> was a daughter of Duke John III of Cleves and the fourth wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII of England</a>. The marriage, arranged in 1540 to ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> with the Protestant-leaning princes of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, was annulled after six months. She remained in England with a generous settlement and outlived the king's other wives.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anne-of-cleves.jpg" alt="Anne of Cleves" width="320" height="441" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hans Holbein the Younger · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anne_de_Cl%C3%A8ves_-_Hans_Holbein_le_Jeune_-_Mus%C3%A9e_du_Louvre_Peintures_INV_1348_;_MR_756_-_version_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Anne was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusseldorf">Düsseldorf</a> in 1515, on either 22 September or 28 June, the second daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-duke-of-cleves">John III, Duke of Cleves</a>, and Maria, Duchess of Jülich-Berg. She grew up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schloss-burg">Schloss Burg</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solingen">Solingen</a>. Her father was influenced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus</a> and followed a moderate course in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, siding with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-league">Schmalkaldic League</a> against Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>. In 1527 she was betrothed to Francis, son of Antoine, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, but the betrothal was cancelled in 1535. Her brother William became Duke of Jülich-Cleves-Berg after their father's death in 1538. She had received no formal education and could read and write only in German.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-cromwell">Thomas Cromwell</a> urged the match on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a>, who sought an alliance with the German Protestants against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. Hans Holbein the Younger painted Anne and her sister Amalia at Düren, and the king, finding the portrait flattering, agreed to the marriage. The treaty was signed on 4 October 1539. Henry first met Anne privately at Rochester on New Year's Day 1540, disguised, and was disappointed; he is reported to have said she was nothing so fair as she had been reported. The wedding took place on 6 January 1540 at Greenwich, performed by Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-cranmer">Thomas Cranmer</a>.</p><p>The marriage was never consummated. Anne was told to leave court on 24 June, and she consented to an annulment, granted on 12 July 1540 on grounds of non-consummation and her earlier pre-contract to Francis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>. Cromwell, the moving force behind the match, was attainted for treason. Anne received a generous settlement, including Richmond Palace and Hever Castle, and became known as the King's Beloved Sister, with precedence over all women in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> except the king's wife and daughters.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Anne stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> for the rest of her life and never returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. She changed religion with the monarch, conforming to Catholic worship in 1540 and again under Mary I, whose coronation procession she joined in 1553. In 1554, after Wyatt's rebellion, she lost royal favour, when the imperial ambassador Simon Renard suspected her of involvement in a plot linked to Elizabeth. At Chelsea Old Manor she dictated her will in July 1557 and died there on 16 July, probably of cancer. She was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a> on 3 August, and she was the last of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a>'s wives to die.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anne of Cleves” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His fall followed the king's disastrous marriage to <b>Anne of Cleves</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-28">28 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>His daughter <b>Anne of Cleves</b> married Henry VIII of England in 1540.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-cleves">Duchy of Cleves</a></li><li><blockquote>John's daughter <b>Anne of Cleves</b> married Henry VIII of England in 1540, and his son William the Rich lost Guelders to Charles V in 1543.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-duchies-of-julich-cleves-berg">United Duchies of Jülich-Cleves-Berg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusseldorf">Düsseldorf</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.23,6.78&amp;year=1542" aria-label="Düsseldorf on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4704 389 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Anne of Cleves"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5154 659h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5154 659h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.23,6.78&amp;year=1542">What was here in 1542? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
